Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below are the columns that will probably matter for answering  
“Which board types were most‑effective on 15‑Sep‑2018 (average score of quick‑play completions)?”

• `event_date` : allows us to keep only 20180915.  
• `event_name` : lets us pick the completion event that represents a finished game / level.  
• `event_params` : holds the individual parameters we need to pull out:  
  – `mode` (expecting the value = “quick_play”)  
  – `board_type` (e.g. “classic”, ”hex”, …)  
  – `score` (the numeric score to average).  

The rest of the columns are unlikely to be used for this specific question.

Below are 10 progressively richer SELECT statements (each ends with `LIMIT 20`) you can run in BigQuery to understand the data and finally produce the required average‑score per board‑type answer.

```sql
-- 1. What event names are present on 15‑Sep‑2018?
SELECT DISTINCT event_name
FROM `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`
WHERE event_date = '20180915'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect a few rows of each event to spot which one looks like
--    a “completion” of the quick‑play mode.
SELECT event_name,
       event_timestamp,
       event_params
FROM `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`
WHERE event_date = '20180915'
ORDER BY event_timestamp
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Look at every distinct parameter key that appears inside event_params
--    to confirm there are `mode`, `board_type`, `score`, etc.
SELECT DISTINCT ep.key
FROM `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`,
     UNNEST(event_params) AS ep
WHERE event_date = '20180915'
LIMIT 20;
